   },
   {
    "quote": "What is the difference between \"to + verb\" and \"-ing form\"?",
    "url": "https://note.com/sashakikuchi/n/nafc2a36a59f0?hl=en",
    "pass": 3
   },
   {
    "quote": "Let's visualize \"to\" as future-oriented and \"-ing\" as past-oriented!",
    "url": "https://note.com/sashakikuchi/n/nafc2a36a59f0?hl=en",
    "pass": 3
   },
   {
    "quote": "Here is one of the questions that often comes up in English learning:",
    "url": "https://note.com/sashakikuchi/n/nafc2a36a59f0?hl=en",
    "pass": 3
   },
   {
    "quote": "\"When I want to say 'to do something,' should I use 'to + verb' or the '-ing' form of the verb?\"",
    "url": "https://note.com/sashakikuchi/n/nafc2a36a59f0?hl=en",
    "pass": 3
   },
   {
    "quote": "Actually, both are the same in that they can be used as a \"noun\" to mean \"doing something.\"",
    "url": "https://note.com/sashakikuchi/n/nafc2a36a59f0?hl=en",
    "pass": 3
   },
   {
    "quote": "there are differences in meaning and usage",
    "url": "https://note.com/sashakikuchi/n/nafc2a36a59f0?hl=en",
    "pass": 3
   },
   {
    "quote": "Especially when they come \"after a verb,\" it is often decided by the verb which one to use, or both may be okay but",
    "url": "https://note.com/sashakikuchi/n/nafc2a36a59f0?hl=en",
    "pass": 3
   }
